# Buyer's-guide body copy per category (tools/category_copy.json)
CATEGORY_COPY = {
 "floor-liners": "<h2>Choosing 3D TPE Floor Liners</h2>\n<p>This is the full-coverage category: moulded trays built against a pattern taken from a specific vehicle, with high side walls that follow the footwell and wrap the sill. They are for importers and distributors selling into markets with real weather, wet boots, road salt or dust, where a flat mat leaves the carpet edge exposed. Sets are typically three or five pieces covering the front row and the rows behind it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm the model year or year range and the generation code, not just the model name.</li>\n<li>Confirm the drive side. Left-hand and right-hand drive use different tooling.</li>\n<li>Decide the piece count - front only, front and second row, or a set that also covers the third row.</li>\n<li>Check whether the retention points in the vehicle floor are moulded into the liner.</li>\n<li>Agree the raised edge height in millimetres, since this changes both containment and carton bulk.</li>\n<li>Ask for the tool reference behind each part number, so a reorder is produced against the same pattern.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Typical products in this range run from high-volume fits such as the <a href=\"/products/toyota-corolla-floor-liners/\">Toyota Corolla liner</a> to deeper trays for SUVs, and each part is grouped by vehicle on pages such as the <a href=\"/shop-by-vehicle/honda/cr-v/\">Honda CR-V fitment page</a>.</p>\n<p>How it differs from the neighbours: flat or semi-universal mats are listed under <a href=\"/all-weather-mats/\">all-weather floor mats</a> and cost less per set, while <a href=\"/trunk-mats/\">trunk mats</a> protect the load floor rather than the footwell. If your market values a heavier, thicker product, look at the <a href=\"/products/floor-liners-4/\">10 mm thickened liner</a> rather than changing category.</p>",
 "all-weather-mats": "<h2>Choosing All-Weather Floor Mats</h2>\n<p>All-weather mats are the flatter end of the range: a waterproof mat with a raised perimeter and a ribbed or lightly patterned face, made for year-round use rather than deep-snow containment. They suit distributors selling to buyers who want a cheaper set than a moulded tray, and to markets where the mat is treated as an everyday consumable rather than a long-life accessory. Most sets in this category are three pieces.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm whether the product is vehicle-specific or semi-universal, and say so on your listing.</li>\n<li>Check the drive side, because a left-hand-drive mat will not sit correctly in a right-hand-drive cabin.</li>\n<li>Agree the perimeter height and the surface pattern, both of which change how the mat feels underfoot.</li>\n<li>Ask about the backing, since an anti-slip underside is what stops the mat creeping forward.</li>\n<li>Confirm set configuration and carton size before quoting retail shipping bands.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples in the range include the <a href=\"/products/byd-atto-all-weather-mats/\">BYD Atto all-weather set</a>, which is supplied in left-hand-drive tooling, and the <a href=\"/products/chevrolet-all-weather-mats/\">Chevrolet all-weather set</a>, produced to order from your model and year. Vehicle-level detail sits on pages such as <a href=\"/shop-by-vehicle/ford/ranger-raptor/\">Ford Ranger Raptor</a>.</p>\n<p>How it differs from the neighbours: if you need high side walls and full trim coverage, that is the <a href=\"/floor-liners/\">3D TPE floor liners</a> category, which costs more per set and occupies more carton volume. Boot protection is a separate purchase under <a href=\"/trunk-mats/\">trunk mats</a>, and a mat with a lift-out top layer belongs to <a href=\"/double-layer-mats/\">double-layer mats</a>.</p>",
 "trunk-mats": "<h2>Choosing Trunk Mats and Cargo Liners</h2>\n<p>Cargo liners cover the load floor rather than the footwell. They are cut to the boot shape with raised edges that contain wet kit, soil, tools or spilled shopping before it soils the trim, and they are sold either as a standalone item or alongside a floor liner programme. Buyers who stock vehicle-specific floor liners usually take the matching cargo liner at the same time, because it is an easy add-on sale to the same customer.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm the body style. A sedan boot floor and an SUV load floor of the same model need different patterns.</li>\n<li>Check whether the load floor is flat or has an under-floor well, and whether you want it covered.</li>\n<li>Agree the raised lip height and whether the liner extends under the rear seat backs.</li>\n<li>Ask for the sheet thickness in millimetres, since thin cargo mats can curl when folded.</li>\n<li>Confirm whether the liner is supplied as one piece or as part of a set.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples include the <a href=\"/products/hyundai-elantra-sedan-3-5mm-trunk-mats/\">Hyundai Elantra sedan cargo liner</a> at 3.5 mm, the made-to-order <a href=\"/products/buick-trunk-mats/\">Buick cargo liner</a>, and the <a href=\"/products/tesla-cybertruck-trunk-mats/\">Cybertruck rear cargo liner</a> for a 2024-2026 fitment listed on the <a href=\"/shop-by-vehicle/tesla/cybertruck/\">Cybertruck vehicle page</a>.</p>\n<p>How it differs from the neighbours: footwell coverage belongs to <a href=\"/floor-liners/\">floor liners</a> and <a href=\"/all-weather-mats/\">all-weather mats</a>, and a cargo mat is not a substitute for either. Two-layer construction, where the top surface lifts out for cleaning, is listed separately under <a href=\"/double-layer-mats/\">double-layer mats</a>.</p>",
 "double-layer-mats": "<h2>Choosing Double-Layer Mats</h2>\n<p>Double-layer mats are a two-part construction: a moulded base that stays in the vehicle and a detachable top layer that lifts out for cleaning. The appeal for a wholesale buyer is the cleaning story - the base is hosed or wiped in place, while the top layer is shaken out or rinsed - and the fact that the set reads as a premium product on a shelf because it looks and feels thicker than a single-layer liner.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirm that the top and base layers of each position are packed together, size for size.</li>\n<li>Agree how the two layers are joined, and how hard the top layer is to remove and refit.</li>\n<li>Decide the base material and the top surface separately, since they can be specified independently.</li>\n<li>Check the set piece count and the carton bulk, as a two-layer set is heavier than a single liner.</li>\n<li>Confirm branding placement on the top layer, and whether it is fixed in the tool or applied later.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The range starts at the <a href=\"/products/double-layer-mats/\">double-layer mat listing</a>, which is made to order from your vehicle and year. Custom printed variants with a defined piece count sit alongside it, such as the <a href=\"/products/floor-liners-6/\">5-piece printed liner</a>, and buyer label printing is offered on products such as the <a href=\"/products/toyota-crown-floor-liners/\">Toyota Crown liner</a>.</p>\n<p>How it differs from the neighbours: single-layer moulded trays with high walls are in <a href=\"/floor-liners/\">3D TPE floor liners</a>, flatter mats are in <a href=\"/all-weather-mats/\">all-weather mats</a>, and load-floor protection is in <a href=\"/trunk-mats/\">trunk mats</a>. Choose double layer when cleaning convenience and shelf presence matter more than the lowest cost per set.</p>"
}


